text:
{  
  "distractors": [  
    {  
      "text": "By increasing the number of people who visit the community hall each week.",  
      "why_wrong": "This is a direct effect of the Repair Café, not a benefit to Ellis & Co. The increased foot traffic at the hall does not necessarily lead to more customers for the hardware shop unless they are specifically directed there."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "By encouraging residents to buy new appliances instead of repairing them.",  
      "why_wrong": "This contradicts the main purpose of the Repair Café, which is to promote repair over replacement. The text clearly shows the café reduces new purchases, so this claim is factually incorrect."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "By reducing the need for customers to repair items at the shop.",  
      "why_wrong": "This misrepresents the role of the café and the shop. The café repairs items, but the shop sells parts; the two are complementary, not competitive. The shop benefits from repairs that require parts, not from fewer repairs."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "By replacing the need for small businesses like Ellis & Co. in the town.",  
      "why_wrong": "This is the opposite of the intended outcome. The town council’s compromise shows they want to support, not replace, local businesses. The café is meant to work with, not against, shops like Ellis & Co."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "By making it easier for customers to return damaged items to the shop for refunds.",  
      "why_wrong": "The Repair Café does not handle returns or refunds. It fixes broken items for free, and there is no mention of a return policy or connection between the café and the shop’s customer service."  
    },  
    {  
      "text": "By allowing the shop to avoid paying taxes on repaired goods.",  
      "why_wrong": "There is no mention of tax implications in the text. This is a completely unrelated and fabricated benefit with no basis in the passage."  
    }  
  ]  
}
